{"chapter": "sites", "node_kind": "example", "parent_tag": "00VL", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$X$", "MATH_0002": "$X_{Zar}$", "MATH_0003": "$X_{Zar}$", "MATH_0004": "$X$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{example-site-topological}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{sheaves-definition-sheaf}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{example}\n\\label{example-sheaves-topological}\n", "suffix": "\\end{example}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:bacc85a0ec7c0b2bcd88d46439e4f35b6fe4dc5ac0bec19e08bb52e70c1ffc10", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:3edc7ba5d0f6484aaa5e3356afcc422ca6e092d31e91e6d62be3f3cd3de41860", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be a topological space. Let <MATH_0002> be the\nsite constructed in Example <REF_0001>.\nThe notion of a sheaf on <MATH_0003> coincides\nwith the notion of a sheaf on <MATH_0004> introduced in\nSheaves, Definition <REF_0002>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:c9d95969c1d04d3b0f90b12260aa57ab4e221d15dbd704d864bb918d9c353742", "unit_id": "tag:00VO:statement"}
{"chapter": "sites", "node_kind": "example", "parent_tag": "00VL", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$X$", "MATH_0002": "$X'_{Zar}$", "MATH_0003": "$X_{Zar}$", "MATH_0004": "$\\{\\emptyset \\to \\emptyset\\}$", "MATH_0005": "$X'_{Zar}$", "MATH_0006": "$X_{Zar}$", "MATH_0007": "$X = \\{p\\}$", "MATH_0008": "$X'_{Zar}$", "MATH_0009": "$\\emptyset, X$", "MATH_0010": "$\\emptyset$", "MATH_0011": "$\\{\\emptyset \\to \\emptyset\\}$", "MATH_0012": "$X$", "MATH_0013": "$\\{X \\to X\\}$", "MATH_0014": "$\\mathcal{F}(\\emptyset)$", "MATH_0015": "$\\mathcal{F}(X)$", "MATH_0016": "$\\mathcal{F}(X) \\to \\mathcal{F}(\\emptyset)$", "MATH_0017": "$X'_{Zar}$", "MATH_0018": "$\\{\\eta, p\\}$", "MATH_0019": "$\\{\\emptyset, \\{\\eta\\}, \\{p, \\eta\\}\\}$", "MATH_0020": "$X'_{Zar}$", "MATH_0021": "$X \\amalg \\{\\eta\\}$", "MATH_0022": "$U \\cup \\{\\eta\\}$", "MATH_0023": "$U \\subset X$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{example-site-topological}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{example}\n\\label{example-topological-wrong}\n", "suffix": "\\end{example}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:1852ef7fc1e14090d0bdd0fb2336e0f2e735e6b6db10641e89c1e9ce2d8e1f4e", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:2537e7edf586ec58b023673fcfa0a027f23d2439b77c3e7071c1213d873739be", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be a topological space. Let us consider the site <MATH_0002> which is\nthe same as the site <MATH_0003> of\nExample <REF_0001> except that\nwe disallow the empty covering of the empty set.\nIn other words, we do allow the covering <MATH_0004>\nbut we do not allow the covering whose index set is empty.\nIt is easy to show that this still defines a site. However,\nwe claim that the sheaves on <MATH_0005> are different\nfrom the sheaves on <MATH_0006>. For example, as an extreme\ncase consider the situation where <MATH_0007> is a singleton.\nThen the objects of <MATH_0008> are <MATH_0009>\nand every